date: 2026-11-09
nixpkgs: nixos-26.05 (see flake.lock; unchanged from 0/2/0)
changes: First version of series 0.3, prompted by agenthub's NATS agent platform (bnox-bg docs/agenthub-nats-plan.md decision 14). (1) New thing-scoped option hardening.network.nats (sibling of outboundHTTPS, self-declarable in .bnox/thing.nix as nats = true). Container things that set it get: slirp4netns networking with allow_host_loopback (--network=slirp4netns:allow_host_loopback=true — podman passes --disable-host-loopback by default, and this is the only working path on podman 5.8.2 rootful: the netavark bridge cannot reach host-loopback services and pasta is rootless-only), so the host loopback is reachable at the fixed slirp gateway 10.0.2.2; IPAddressAllow 10.0.2.2 + 127.0.0.1 under the standard deny-any filter (both addresses required: the cgroup-bpf filter also sees slirp4netns's host-side relay connect to 127.0.0.1 — allowing 10.0.2.2 alone deadlocks as connect timeouts); NATS_URL=nats://10.0.2.2:4222 in the container env; an implied nats_seed slot (/var/lib/bnox/secrets/<thing>/nats_seed, an env file containing NATS_SEED=<nkey seed>, host-written at runtime, watched by the usual path unit so seed rotation restarts the thing); and slirp4netns added to podman extraPackages. host-install things that set it get the same slot plus NATS_URL=nats://127.0.0.1:4222 (host netns). (3) Fix: 0/2/0's container-thing units could never start a container. Rootful podman is a container *runner*, and the standard thing bundle (a process sandbox) is incompatible with it in six independent ways, each verified live on agenthub (podman 5.8.2, systemd 260.2): ProtectHome=true fatals podman's config lookup (EACCES statting $HOME/.config/containers); the empty CapabilityBoundingSet kills pause-process creation (EPERM writing uid_map); ProtectSystem=strict without /var/lib/containers + /var/cache/containers in ReadWritePaths denies podman's storage locks; ProcSubset=pid masks /proc/sys (podman reads cap_last_cap); ProtectControlGroups=read-only blocks crun's container cgroup creation; ProtectHostname blocks the container's sethostname. PrivateDevices also masks /dev/net/tun (needed by slirp4netns, hence by the nats knob), and SystemCallFilter/@system-service excludes syscalls crun's child needs. Container-thing units therefore get a new runnerBundle in hardening.nix instead of the thing bundle: podman itself is the payload's sandbox, so the unit keeps only the boundaries that still bind (ProtectSystem=strict with podman's storage paths writable, ProtectHome=read-only, PrivateTmp, RestrictAddressFamilies AF_UNIX/AF_INET/AF_INET6/AF_NETLINK — netlink for netavark's iptables, ProtectKernelModules/Logs, ProtectClock, ProtectProc=invisible) and drops the process-level lockdown (caps, syscalls, nnp, PrivateDevices, kernel-tunables/cgroups/hostname, ProcSubset). The load-bearing boundary — IPAddressDeny=any + IPAddressAllow — is kept and demonstrably still scopes container traffic (the egress cgroup-bpf filter sees the payload's connections). The pipeline build units (podman load/save) keep their own profile with ProtectHome=read-only (the same podman config-lookup fix; load needs none of the runner relaxations). No 0/2/0 container thing ever started, so this fixes a class that had no working victims. (4) Fix: 0/2/0's port-scoped IPAddressAllow entries (":443" for container outboundHTTPS, ":443 :53" for host-install) are load-invalid on systemd 260 (260.2, the pinned nixpkgs): IPAddressAllow/Deny take address/prefix only, no ports — every outboundHTTPS container and every host-install thing would fail to start the moment those lists were exercised (latent: none ever had). outboundHTTPS and host-install now allow "any" — honest degradation, since an egress-allowing filter without port scoping is no filter; loopback-only units pin per-unit allowlists in host config (visible overrides). (5) Fix: 0/2/0 set the container payload's --user to the thing's user name, but podman resolves names only against the image's /etc/passwd — dockerTools images carry no per-thing users, so every container thing failed with "unable to find user"; numeric uids cannot be used either (thing users get their uid assigned at activation, not eval). Container payloads therefore run as container root; the container itself is the payload's isolation boundary, and the thing's system user remains exactly what it always actually was — the host-side boundary: owner of /var/lib/<thing> state and the secrets slots. Things that want a non-root payload user bake one into their own image (dockerTools runAsRoot) — extension path, not bnox's. All networking facts verified live on agenthub (podman 5.8.2, systemd 260.2, nixpkgs 8623c4c) plus a full container round-trip against the locked-down broker through the generated thing unit.
